      "image": "assets/malware-1.webp",
      "text": "MALWARE ANALYSIS REPORT\n[SUMMARY]\nThis report provides a technical analysis of executable sample\n95c21c7542ced5450ff32d9d077f454c763443cacd821d45bd14c30b87d9e8f3.exe. Static and dynamic\nanalysis identified a packed and obfuscated 64-bit .NET executable that modified Windows Defender settings,\nused hidden PowerShell with execution policy bypass, created persistence artifacts, and dropped a secondary\npayload. Windows Defender classified the dropped payload as Trojan:MSIL/NegaStealer.AB!MTB during local\ntesting. Post analysis validation against MalwareBazaar identifies the submitted sample as RemcosRAT, with\nmultiple linked sandboxes also reporting Remcos related behavior.\n[THREAT SEVERITY] HIGH\nThe sample demonstrates high-risk behavior including .NET packing/obfuscation, attempts to modify Defender policy,\nhidden PowerShell execution, and persistence through registry and scheduled-task artifacts.\n[STATIC ANALYSIS & METADATA]\nStatic property inspection of the binary confirms that the executable is compiled as a 64-bit Portable Executable\n(PE32+) targeted for the Microsoft .NET Framework (v4.7.2).\nFile Property\nValue / Detail\nFile Name\n95c21c7542ced5450ff32d9d077f454c763443cacd821d45bd14c30b87d9e8f3.exe\nSHA-256 Hash\n95C21C7542CED5450FF32D9D077F454C763443CACD821D45BD14C30B87D9E8F3\nFile Size\n1.37 MB (1,444,864 bytes)\nCompiler / Language\nVB.NET / Microsoft Intermediate Language (MSIL)\nOriginal Filename\nZlvc.exe\nEmbedded Product Name\nVirtualMachine26\nEmbedded Company Name\nHyperCore Labs (embedded metadata)\nCompile Stamp\nThu Aug 27 03:46:08 2026 UTC\nPDB Debug Path\nC:\\Users\\Administrator\\Desktop\\Client\\Temp\\NOCvnnitRK\\src\\obj\\Debug\\Zlvc.pdb\n[Entropy & Packing Indicators]\nDetect It Easy and PeStudio showed unusually high entropy in key areas of the Portable Executable (PE).\nThe .text section measured 7.944 entropy and represented most of the file, while an embedded resource measured\n7.995 entropy (approximately 1.32 MB). High entropy is consistent with compression, packing, or obfuscation and\ncan make static inspection more difficult. External sandbox validation later identified SmartAssembly .NET packing\nfor this sample, supporting the local packing/obfuscation assessment.\nStatic & Dynamic Analysis Report | D. Wynkoop\nSeptember 5, 2026\n"
    },
    {
      "image": "assets/malware-2.webp",
      "text": "[DYNAMIC BEHAVIOR & HOST MODIFICATIONS]\nUpon dynamic execution within an isolated Windows analysis virtual machine, Process Monitor captured specific host\ntampering actions designed to weaken security configurations and degrade operating system defenses.\n[ZoneMap Configuration Changes]\nProcess Monitor captured changes to Windows Internet Settings ZoneMap values. Specifically, the sample modified\nthe following values under the registry path\nHKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Internet Settings\\ZoneMap\\:\n• ProxyBypass: 1 (Maps proxy-bypass sites into the Local Intranet zone)\n• IntranetName: 1 (Includes local intranet sites not otherwise assigned)\n• UNCAsIntranet: 1 (Maps UNC network paths into the Local Intranet zone)\n• AutoDetect: 0 (Disables automatic intranet detection)\nThese values change how Windows classifies local and intranet resources. Proxy-bypass sites and UNC paths may be\ntreated as Local Intranet content under this configuration. The analysis confirmed the registry changes but did not\ndemonstrate proxy bypass traffic, unauthorized script execution, or lateral movement.\n[Antivirus Defense Evasion]\nProcess Monitor and Defender alerts show an attempt to modify Microsoft Defender policy. The sample accessed\nand attempted to write the following value:\nHKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender\\DisableAntiSpyware\nThe attempted change is consistent with defense evasion. Defender detected the modification during the analysis;\nthe local run did not establish that real time protection was successfully disabled or that C2 occurred.\n[PERSISTENCE MECHANISMS]\nRegshot comparison data showed two persistence-related artifacts created after execution:\nThe artifacts are documented as observed; intended trigger behavior was not independently validated.\nType\nTarget Registry/Task Path\nDetails / Command Line\nRegistry Run Key\nHKU\\S-1-5-21-...\\Software\\Microsoft\\\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\XoI\nLaunches PowerShell with -ExecutionPolicy Bypass\nand a hidden window.\nScheduled Task\n\\SoftLanding (GUID: {00B2430C-BAA4-45FD-9FBA-A23DC18FCBA7})\nCreates a scheduled task artifact named SoftLanding;\ntrigger behavior was not independently validated.\n[Obfuscated Run-Key PowerShell Command Analysis]\nThe registry Run value Xoi invokes PowerShell with flags that suppress profiles, bypass execution policy, and hide\nthe window:\npowershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -WindowStyle Hidden -Command ...\nThe command also contains Base64-encoded strings. Decoding the observed strings revealed:\nEncoded String\nDecoded Value\nTechnical Action\nU3RhcnQtU2xlZXA=\nStart-Sleep\nIntroduces an execution delay that may reduce\nbehavior observed in short analysis windows.\nQzpcVXNlcnNcV2luZG93c1VzZXJc\nQXBwRGF0YVxSb2FtaW5nXFhvbC\n5leG== (Partially obfuscated string)\nC:\\Users\\WindowsUser\\AppData\\Roaming\\XoI.exe\nReferences the dropped executable for later\nexecution.\nHKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender\\DisableAntiVirus\n"
    },
    {
      "image": "assets/malware-3.webp",
      "text": "[DROPPED PAYLOADS & ENDPOINT DETECTION ALERTS]\nDuring dynamic execution, the sample created or referenced a dropped executable under the user Roaming profile.\nWindows Defender generated the following alerts during the analysis.\nSecurity Alert\nClassification\nAction Taken / Context\nVirTool:Win32/DefenderTamperingRestore\nSevere\n(Active/Quarantined)\nTriggered by the attempted modification of the\nDisableAntiSpyware value in Microsoft Defender policies.\nVirTool:PowerShell/Magnib.A\nSevere\n(Active/Quarantined)\nTriggered by the obfuscated Run-key PowerShell\ncommand; Defender/AMSI detected the script content.\nTrojan:MSIL/NegaStealer.AB!MTB\nSevere (Quarantined)\nLocal Defender classification for the dropped executable payload\n‘XoI.exe’ under 'C:\\Users\\WindowsUser\\AppData\\Roaming\\'.\n[Incident Response]\n- Host Isolation: Immediately isolate affected endpoints to prevent further network communication.\n- Process Remediation: Terminate the malicious sample process, the dropped payload, and any associated suspicious\nPowerShell processes after preserving evidence.\n- File Cleanup: Quarantine/remove C:\\Users\\WindowsUser\\AppData\\Roaming\\Xol.exe after evidence collection.\n- Registry/Task Restoration: Remove XoI and SoftLanding artifacts and restore modified Defender/ZoneMap\nsettings from known-good values.\n- EDR/AV Sweep: Scan for Remcos, NegaStealer detections, and the observed behavioral indicators.\n[Post Analysis]\nPost analysis comparison with MalwareBazaar was performed after the local investigation.\nMalwareBazaar lists the submitted sample as RemcosRAT; the SHA-256 and file size match this sample.\nLinked Joe Sandbox, Hatching Triage, CERT.PL MWDB, Threatray, and VMRay results also identify Remcos.\nTriage reports SmartAssembly packing, PowerShell execution, Run-key persistence, and Defender policy changes.\nIt also extracts Remcos C2 configuration, including www.dmgl-international.xyz:2404 and backup domains.\nThese external results corroborate several local behaviors but are not treated as locally observed network activity.\nThe windows analysis VM was intentionally Internet-isolated, so no external C2 communications were confirmed locally.\n"
    }
